{{/*
  Ticket template. Go text/template over pkg/ticket.TemplateData.

  The FIRST line must be "Summary: ..."; then a blank line; then the description
  body. Keeping both in one file means the whole wording of a ticket lives in one
  place, editable by whoever owns the process rather than in Go.

  Available:
    .ServiceName .Images .ImageCount .GroupNoun .Refs .Priority
    .Source .SourcePath - the shared change target, present on grouped tickets too
    .Accounts .Namespaces .Teams .WorkloadCount
    .CriticalCount .HighCount .ProviderAssessed
    .FixableCriticals (.ID .CVSS .EPSS .FixedVersion .KEV) .MaxEPSS .KnownExploited
    .Urgent   - the CVEs the proposed change was measured to clear (exploited,
                or EPSS at or above jira.urgentEPSS, with a fix), each with .Why
                .Where .Action .Package .FixedVersion .Reference. ONLY what the
                change clears: empty when nothing was measured, which is not a
                claim that it clears nothing
    .UrgentCleared .UrgentUnknown .UrgentRemaining .UrgentAllCleared - kept
                for older templates; with .Urgent holding only cleared CVEs
                they are len(.Urgent), 0, 0 and "non-empty"
    .Upgrade  - the single version move, or NIL when the grouped images do not
                share one target version (.Kind .Name .Current .Latest .Managed
                .Source .SourcePath .Direct)
    .Fixes    - images updated as a CONSEQUENCE of .Upgrades, not work in
                themselves (chain-merged tickets only)
    .Upgrades - the version move(s) to apply, one entry per image the change
                moves (.Ref .Repo .Current .Latest .Source .SourcePath .Managed
                .Direct). .Current/.Latest are the image's own tags; when a Helm
                chart owns the version, .Chart .ChartCurrent .ChartLatest name it,
                and .Latest is empty if the target chart's tag could not be read.
                .LatestFrom is "appVersion" when the chart's values left the tag
                empty and its appVersion stands in ("values" when read from a
                tag); .LatestRepo is set when the chart moves the image to a
                different repository (for example another registry)
                .Managed "operator" with .Chart set means an operator picks the
                image's tag at runtime and the change is the operator's chart
    .Packages - package moves the images' own build has to make (.Name
                .Ecosystem .Path .Current .Fixed .CVEs .CVEList): beside a
                rebuild that leaves them, or the whole change when
                .PackageOnly is true. .Upgrade.Kind is "package" when the
                whole group moves one package to one version
    .PackageOnly - the change is moving .Packages and nothing else: no newer
                base exists, or the one on offer clears none of what made
                this actionable, so a rebuild will not fix it
    .SummaryCVEs - the CVEs the package moves clear, for a summary. A ticket
                whose summary names CVEs is closed once none of them is
                reported on its images any more
    .Disambiguator - set ONLY when another ticket in this run renders the same
                summary, naming what makes this one different. Empty otherwise,
                so putting it in a summary costs nothing on the tickets that
                have nothing to be confused with.

  This is an EXAMPLE. Edit it to match how your teams actually work.
*/ -}}
{{- if .PackageOnly -}}
Summary: {{ with .Upgrade }}Upgrade {{ .Name }} to {{ .Latest }}{{ else }}Upgrade {{ len .Packages }} packages{{ end }} in {{ .ServiceName }} ({{ .SummaryCVEs }}){{ with .Disambiguator }} ({{ . }}){{ end }}
{{- else if .Upgrade -}}
Summary: Upgrade {{ .ServiceName }} to {{ .Upgrade.Latest }}{{ with .Disambiguator }} ({{ . }}){{ end }}
{{- else -}}
Summary: Upgrade {{ .ServiceName }} {{ .GroupNoun }} ({{ .ImageCount }}) to their latest versions{{ with .Disambiguator }} ({{ . }}){{ end }}
{{- end }}

**Business objective/requirements**

{{ if .PackageOnly -}}
Change the build of {{ .ServiceName }} to clear known container vulnerabilities in packages it installs or declares. They are not in the base image, so rebuilding on a newer base will not fix this.
{{- else if .Upgrade -}}
Upgrade {{ .ServiceName }} from {{ .Upgrade.Current }} to {{ .Upgrade.Latest }} to clear known container vulnerabilities.
{{- else -}}
Upgrade the {{ .ImageCount }} images below to clear known container vulnerabilities. {{ if .Source }}They are grouped because one change covers them all.{{ else }}They are grouped because they are managed together; each is bumped in its own place, listed below.{{ end }}
{{- end }}

**Technical Requirements & Actions**

{{ range .Upgrades }}* {{ .Repo }}: {{ if and .Chart (eq .Managed "operator") }}{{ .Current }} (chosen at runtime by the operator the {{ .Chart }} chart installs, which moves {{ .ChartCurrent }} -> {{ .ChartLatest }}; the tag the operator picks is only known once it runs, so check it after the upgrade){{ else if .Chart }}{{ .Current }}{{ if .Latest }} -> {{ .Latest }}{{ if eq .LatestFrom "appVersion" }} (appVersion){{ end }}{{ end }} (set by the {{ .Chart }} chart, which moves {{ .ChartCurrent }} -> {{ .ChartLatest }}{{ if not .Latest }}; the tag it deploys could not be read, so check it after the upgrade{{ else if .LatestRepo }}; the image moves to {{ .LatestRepo }}:{{ .Latest }} (from {{ .Repo }}){{ end }}){{ else }}{{ .Current }}{{ if .Latest }} -> {{ .Latest }}{{ end }}{{ end }}{{ if not .Direct }} ({{ if eq .Managed "helm" }}version owned by its Helm chart, so bump it there{{ else }}version owned by the {{ .Managed }}, so bump it there{{ end }}){{ end }}{{ if and (not $.Source) .Source }}, change in {{ .Source }}{{ if .SourcePath }} path {{ .SourcePath }}{{ end }}{{ end }}
{{ end -}}
{{- with .Fixes }}
This also updates the following, which are not bumped directly:

{{ range . }}* {{ .Repo }}: {{ .Current }}{{ if .Latest }} -> {{ .Latest }}{{ if eq .LatestFrom "appVersion" }} (appVersion){{ end }}{{ if .LatestRepo }}; the image moves to {{ .LatestRepo }}:{{ .Latest }} (from {{ .Repo }}){{ end }}{{ end }}
{{ end -}}
{{- end }}
{{- if and .Upgrade (eq .Upgrade.Kind "base") }}* Rebuild {{ .ServiceName }}: it is built on top of {{ .Upgrade.Name }}, and moving that to {{ .Upgrade.Latest }} is what the rebuild picks up.{{ if and .Urgent (not .Packages) }} That alone clears every CVE under "Done means".{{ end }}
{{ if .BuildRepo }}* Rebuild from {{ .BuildRepo }}.
{{ end }}* Redeploy the new image everywhere it runs, listed below. A rebuilt image fixes nothing until it is running.
{{ else }}{{- if .Source }}* Change target: {{ .Source }}{{ if .SourcePath }} (path: {{ .SourcePath }}){{ end }}
{{ end }}{{ end }}
{{- with .Packages }}{{ if not $.PackageOnly }}* The rebuild does not touch these: the build adds them, so move them there as well.
{{ end }}{{ range . }}* {{ .Name }}{{ with .Path }} (in {{ . }}){{ end }}: {{ with .Current }}{{ . }}{{ else }}installed version{{ end }} -> {{ .Fixed }}, clearing {{ .CVEList }}
{{ end }}{{ if $.PackageOnly }}* Make the change in the Dockerfile or dependency manifest{{ with $.BuildRepo }} in {{ . }}{{ end }}, rebuild, and redeploy everywhere it runs, listed below. A rebuild on a newer base image alone will not fix this.
{{ end }}{{ end }}
